        <div id="why-oil-stains-penetrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Oil Stains Penetrate Concrete (And Why They're So Stubborn)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete looks solid, but under a microscope it's a <strong>porous network of interconnected capillaries</strong> formed as the cement paste cures. Those pores are exactly what allow oil, grease, and other petroleum-based fluids to soak in rather than sit on the surface the way they would on glass or sealed tile. The moment a drop of motor oil lands on bare concrete, capillary action begins pulling it downward and outward, spreading it through the pore structure faster than most people realize — a fresh spill can penetrate meaningfully deep within the first 30-60 minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Time is the single biggest factor</strong> in how hard a stain is to remove. A spill that's wiped up within minutes may leave only a faint mark, because most of the oil never had the chance to travel far into the pores. A spill that sits for days, weeks, or years behaves very differently: the oil continues migrating deeper, and UV exposure and oxidation gradually change its chemistry, causing it to <strong>harden and cross-link</strong> within the concrete's pore structure. This is why a fresh drip under a car often wipes away easily while the decade-old stain in the same spot seems to be a permanent part of the floor — chemically, it almost is.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete porosity itself varies</strong> too. Older, unsealed driveways and garage floors — the most common oil-stain victims — tend to be more porous than newer concrete poured with modern low-permeability mixes, and they have no sealer film to slow oil penetration in the first place. This combination of high porosity, long dwell time, and oxidized, hardened oil is exactly what the <strong>best oil stain remover for concrete</strong> has to overcome: it must penetrate as deep as the oil did, chemically break it back down or absorb it, and hold it in suspension long enough to rinse it out rather than let it re-settle into the pores.
          </p>

