        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Removers -->
        <div id="types-of-removers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Oil Stain Removers: Which One Is Right for You?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every oil stain remover works the same way, and picking the wrong category for your situation is the most common reason people give up thinking "nothing removes oil stains from concrete." Here are the four main approaches, and where each one shines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Absorbent Poultice Powders</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Oil-Dri Absorbent Granules</strong> use clay or diatomaceous earth to physically pull liquid oil out of the concrete's surface through capillary action before it has a chance to cure into a permanent stain. This is the <strong>best first-response tool</strong> for a fresh spill — pour it on immediately, let it sit for a few hours to overnight, then sweep it away. Poultices don't remove staining that has already set; they only prevent it from getting worse. Best for: the first 24 hours after any spill, and as ongoing insurance in a garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Enzyme / Bio-Based Degreasers</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Chomp! Grease & Oil Stain Remover</strong> uses live bacterial cultures and enzymes that consume hydrocarbons, converting oil into water and carbon dioxide rather than just suspending it in a cleaning solution. These are the <strong>gentlest effective option</strong> — no fumes, no caustic chemicals — but they need the oil to still be somewhat accessible to bacteria, which means they perform best on stains that are days or weeks old rather than years old. Best for: fresh-to-moderate spills where you want a low-odor, family- and pet-safe treatment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solvent-Based Degreasers</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Terminator-HSD</strong>, <strong>Purple Power</strong>, and <strong>Oil Eater</strong> use surfactants and, in the heavier-duty formulas, solvent packages to chemically emulsify and break down oil so it can be scrubbed and rinsed away. This is the <strong>largest and most versatile category</strong>, spanning gentle water-based cleaners suitable for decorative concrete up to industrial-strength formulas built for years-old, oxidized grease. Best for: the majority of driveway and garage floor oil stains, from moderate to severe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure-Washer-Only Approaches</h3>
          <p>
            Many homeowners try blasting an oil stain with a pressure washer alone, with no chemical treatment first. In our testing, this consistently <strong>underperformed every dedicated cleaner</strong> on this list — water pressure removes loose surface dirt and grime effectively, but it does nothing to chemically break the bond between oil and the concrete's pore structure. Pressure washing is an excellent <strong>final rinse step</strong> after a degreaser has done its job, but as a standalone method it left visible staining behind in every test we ran, even on relatively fresh spills.
          </p>

