        <div id="how-to-remove-oil-stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Remove Oil Stains From Concrete: Fresh vs. Old Stains
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right removal process depends heavily on how long the oil has been sitting. Treating a fresh drip the same way you'd treat a five-year-old stain wastes time and product — and treating an old stain the way you'd treat a fresh one guarantees disappointment. Here's the process for each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Removing a Fresh Oil Stain (Same Day to a Few Days Old)</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 1: Absorb immediately.</strong> Cover the spill generously with <strong>Oil-Dri Absorbent Granules</strong> or plain kitty litter/sawdust as soon as you notice it. Leave it undisturbed for several hours or overnight to pull as much liquid oil out of the pores as possible before it can penetrate further.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 2: Sweep away the saturated absorbent</strong> and dispose of it properly — never down a storm drain.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 3: Apply an enzyme cleaner or water-based degreaser.</strong> Spray <strong>Chomp!</strong> or <strong>Oil Eater</strong> over the remaining stain. Let it dwell for 10-30 minutes as directed. For enzyme cleaners, minimal or no scrubbing is needed. For degreasers, light agitation with a stiff brush helps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 4: Rinse thoroughly</strong> with a garden hose or pressure washer set to a moderate fan pattern. Check the area once dry — a fresh spill treated promptly should show little to no visible mark.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Removing an Old, Set-In Oil Stain (Weeks to Years Old)</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 1: Choose a heavy-duty product.</strong> Reach for <strong>Terminator-HSD</strong> or <strong>Purple Power</strong> — enzyme cleaners and light degreasers generally can't penetrate hardened, oxidized oil on their own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 2: Apply liberally and extend the dwell time.</strong> Saturate the stain fully and let the product sit for <strong>20-30 minutes</strong>, or overnight for the most severe staining, so the chemicals have time to soften the hardened oil residue.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 3: Scrub aggressively.</strong> Use a stiff nylon or wire-bristle push broom, working in a circular motion, to physically break up and lift the softened residue. This step matters more for old stains than fresh ones — chemistry alone often isn't enough.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step 4: Rinse and assess.</strong> Rinse thoroughly with a pressure washer. If the stain is still visible, repeat the full process — most years-old stains need <strong>two to three treatments</strong> spaced a day apart before the discoloration is fully gone. Some very deep, very old stains may never fully disappear, but repeat treatments consistently produce dramatic improvement even when they don't achieve 100% removal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Preventing Future Oil Stains -->
        <div id="preventing-future-stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Preventing Future Oil Stains: Seal After You Clean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Removing an existing oil stain is only half the battle — if you don't do anything differently afterward, the same spot will simply stain again the next time something leaks or drips. The single most effective long-term prevention step is to <strong>seal the concrete</strong> immediately after it's fully clean and completely dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quality <strong>penetrating or film-forming concrete sealer</strong> dramatically reduces the porosity that allows oil to soak in during the critical first 30-60 minutes after a spill. On sealed concrete, a fresh drip sits closer to the surface rather than wicking downward, which means you have far more time to notice and wipe it up before it becomes a stain at all — turning what used to be a permanent mark into a five-minute paper-towel job. This is especially worthwhile for garage floors where a vehicle is parked regularly, and for driveways where oil changes or equipment maintenance happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond sealing, a few simple habits go a long way: keep a <strong>drip pan or cardboard</strong> under any vehicle with a known slow leak, wipe up spills the moment you see them rather than waiting for a weekend cleaning session, and keep a container of <strong>absorbent granules</strong> on a garage shelf so you can respond within minutes rather than hours. Combined with a sealed surface, these habits mean most future spills never get the chance to become the kind of stain that needs a heavy-duty degreaser to remove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best oil stain remover for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oil Eater Original Cleaner Degreaser is the best overall oil stain remover for concrete because it handles both fresh spills and older, set-in stains with a single water-based, biodegradable formula that's safe on decorative and stamped concrete. For a years-old, deeply penetrated stain, Terminator-HSD Concrete Cleaner is the stronger, contractor-grade choice. For a spill caught within a day or two, Chomp! Grease & Oil Stain Remover uses enzymes to fully digest the oil with no scrubbing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove old oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Old, set-in oil stains require a stronger approach than fresh spills. Apply a heavy-duty degreaser like Terminator-HSD or Purple Power Industrial Strength directly to the dry stain, let it dwell for 20-30 minutes (or overnight for severe cases) so the chemicals can penetrate the hardened, oxidized oil, then scrub vigorously with a stiff nylon brush before rinsing thoroughly with a hose or pressure washer. Multiple treatments spaced a day apart are often needed for stains that have been set for years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Dawn dish soap remove oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dawn dish soap can help with fresh, light oil stains because its surfactants break the surface tension of oil, similar to how it cuts grease on dishes. However, it lacks the concentrated solvent or enzymatic power needed for set-in or older stains, and it isn't formulated for the dwell-and-scrub process that works best on concrete. For anything beyond a very light, fresh spot, a purpose-built degreaser like Oil Eater or Chomp! will significantly outperform dish soap.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will WD-40 remove oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                WD-40 can sometimes help loosen an oil stain because its solvent carrier can re-liquefy hardened oil, but it isn't designed as a stain remover and typically needs to be followed immediately with a real degreaser and hot water rinse, or it will simply add more oil to the surface. It's a supplementary trick at best, not a substitute for a dedicated concrete degreaser like the products in this guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should degreaser sit on concrete before rinsing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dwell time depends on the product and stain age. Water-based degreasers like Oil Eater typically need 5-20 minutes on fresh stains and up to 20-30 minutes on older ones. Enzyme cleaners like Chomp! work well with a 20-30 minute dwell on fresh spills. Heavy-duty solvent products like Terminator-HSD may need 25-30 minutes, or even overnight, on years-old stains. Always scrub with a stiff brush just before rinsing to help lift loosened oil out of the concrete's pores.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use trisodium phosphate (TSP) safely on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, TSP is safe for concrete surfaces and is an effective, inexpensive degreaser when mixed with hot water at the recommended ratio. However, TSP is caustic, so you should wear rubber gloves and eye protection, avoid runoff onto grass or garden beds, and rinse thoroughly after use. Some states and municipalities restrict retail sale of true TSP due to phosphate runoff regulations, so check local rules before purchasing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does pressure washing alone remove oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pressure washing alone rarely removes a genuine oil stain from concrete because water pressure doesn't break the chemical bond between oil and the concrete's porous surface — it mostly just cleans loose dirt and surface debris. For real results, apply a degreaser or enzyme cleaner first, let it dwell and break down the oil, scrub the area, and then use the pressure washer as the rinsing step rather than the primary cleaning method.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal concrete after removing an oil stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Once a stain is fully removed and the concrete is completely dry, applying a penetrating or film-forming concrete sealer creates a barrier that keeps future oil, grease, and fluid spills from soaking into the pores in the first place. This makes any future spill dramatically easier to wipe up before it ever becomes a permanent stain, and it's the single best long-term prevention step you can take after a successful cleanup.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

